{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>You shall not pervert justice.</b> A warning to the judge, or a warning to each and every one of Israel, not to appoint a judge unless he is expert and worthy, for this is a cause to pervert justice; and if one did so it is as if he planted an asherah — therefore Scripture juxtaposes to it: ״לא תטע לך אשרה כל עץ״." "<b>And in this parashah</b> you will find six negative commandments. And they said in the Midrash Wayikra Rabbah: six steps did the throne of Shelomo have, as it is written (II Chronicles 9:18): ״ושש מעלות לכסא״, and the herald would stand before the throne of Shelomo: when he ascended the first step, the herald would say, ״לא תטה משפט״; the second, the herald would say, ״לא תכיר פנים״; the third, the herald would say, ״לא תקח שחד״; the fourth, the herald would say, ״לא תטע״; the fifth, he would say, ״ולא תקים״; the sixth, ״לא תזבח״. Thus far." "<b>You shall not show favoritism.</b> That a rich man should not sit while a poor man stands, nor should he be soft toward this one and harsh toward that one, for his claims become blocked." "<b>And you shall not take a bribe.</b> Even to judge truthfully, for once he has accepted the bribe he becomes blind in judgment. And our Rabbis of blessed memory said: what is שחד (shohad)? That it is חד (one), meaning that the giver and the receiver have become one, and he no longer sees any liability in him. And one who receives a bribe is called wicked, as it is written (Proverbs 17:23): ״שחד מחיק רשע יקח״, and it is written (ibid. 21:14): ״ושחד בחיק חמה עזה״, and it is written (ibid. 17:8): ״אבן חן השחד בעיני בעליו״, and our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: why is it likened to a stone? Because wherever it falls, it shatters."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:48:54.758782525Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}